hostapd: ctrl_iface replies must not block

Events to attached monitors go out with MSG_DONTWAIT, but replies to
requests use a blocking sendto(). A client that uses one socket for both
(libwifi/wifimngr on every MLD link socket) and stops reading for a while
fills its receive buffer with events; the reply to its next request then
blocks hostapd in sock_alloc_send_pskb until the client reads again. All
APs and links of that hostapd stop serving meanwhile: on 2026-09-18 x8
hostapd hung ~3 min (ubus silent, a mesh child could not authenticate).
Send replies with MSG_DONTWAIT too; a client that is not reading loses
the reply and times out instead of freezing the AP.

--- a/hostapd/ctrl_iface.c	2026-09-18 19:36:50.168117011 +0200
+++ b/hostapd/ctrl_iface.c	2026-09-18 19:36:50.172311015 +0200
@@ -7396,7 +7396,7 @@
 
 	reply = os_malloc(reply_size);
 	if (reply == NULL) {
-		if (sendto(sock, "FAIL\n", 5, 0, (struct sockaddr *) &from,
+		if (sendto(sock, "FAIL\n", 5, MSG_DONTWAIT, (struct sockaddr *) &from,
 			   fromlen) < 0) {
 			wpa_printf(MSG_DEBUG, "CTRL: sendto failed: %s",
 				   strerror(errno));
@@ -7446,7 +7446,7 @@
 #ifdef CONFIG_CTRL_IFACE_UDP
 done:
 #endif /* CONFIG_CTRL_IFACE_UDP */
-	if (sendto(sock, reply, reply_len, 0, (struct sockaddr *) &from,
+	if (sendto(sock, reply, reply_len, MSG_DONTWAIT, (struct sockaddr *) &from,
 		   fromlen) < 0) {
 		wpa_printf(MSG_DEBUG, "CTRL: sendto failed: %s",
 			   strerror(errno));
@@ -7572,7 +7572,7 @@
 
 	reply = os_malloc(reply_size);
 	if (!reply) {
